  "decision_conditions": [
    "The oak tree is currently hosting gall wasps, which are a natural part of its ecosystem and not a sign of disease. These insects are commonly found on healthy oak trees and cause minimal impact on the tree’s overall health or growth. Because of this, efforts to control them are typically unnecessary and not recommended. Some types of gall wasps can affect the development of acorns, occasionally leading to reduced acorn production in certain years. However, this does not pose a long-term threat to oak populations, as acorn yields naturally vary and are often abundant in years when gall wasp activity is low."
